[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d555c6-f414-5c97-8c5b-91e3fadce1c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{201\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241229} {:answer/id #uuid "47b3512a-8644-536f-91fd-94aedcce3572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{232\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241829} {:answer/id #uuid "c494145e-1533-577e-98b8-aedb8fd6d800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242910} {:answer/id #uuid "cca3b680-e248-5661-9f3c-fb83e186fbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242978} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f8f60b-0948-5eed-a605-858c3df2ebe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{116\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47b3512a-8644-536f-91fd-94aedcce3572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{232\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241829} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adf3bb7-d19c-519f-95f3-7085a2d55a4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca31b615-018c-5f6b-b173-7f397965d43a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109413" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2})={x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=x.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{2{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{2(4)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(4)^{3/2}}{3}-(\\frac{2(1)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3})] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{116}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{232\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26347220-3d03-59ee-8e7b-cb61d02c8618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536341} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce04388-54d1-53d5-91e9-530b14e04a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536379} {:answer/id #uuid "58346a39-6f7b-5629-aba6-151206e88d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536441} {:answer/id #uuid "d82f7750-cdb5-5af6-a415-d4526008d845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536624} {:answer/id #uuid "e52ce18d-55d4-5980-93b5-05d516aca7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-5}{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52ce18d-55d4-5980-93b5-05d516aca7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-5}{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536645} :answer-field/id #uuid "2122b91f-e6b1-53a7-bc39-72f4271ba65f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49aa78c4-2d01-5b28-b29f-36722ae18536" :question/math-academy-id "q-109424" :question/problem "![](images/58/5806f57e41eeb5548b0c041533adf3952dd7f35236121f9056c57f03c2854ddc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{1-x}$ that lies in the first quadrant between $x=0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-x} & =0 \\\\ 1-x & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{1-x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(1-x)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{\\frac{5-4x}{4(1-x)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-5}{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242455a6-be88-54f8-ad2a-073aed9219d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244630} {:answer/id #uuid "25680994-f4bd-54a1-a482-ab99f94a76d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244645} {:answer/id #uuid "47ddc665-80e7-527f-a69c-522a305cffe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244954} {:answer/id #uuid "63c479b4-bb47-56ba-b47e-40fa34876d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245188} {:answer/id #uuid "d716a18a-4a0d-5bb7-af54-448947c53223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25680994-f4bd-54a1-a482-ab99f94a76d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244645} :answer-field/id #uuid "7687ea4b-b2b9-5647-8d8a-b1fe78f2a22e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d9b2c67-e61f-5dae-a147-dc5eaf3565f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-109594" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2x+3y$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2x+3y,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4+9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6a3e1b6-3e3a-58d9-b55e-673dc95b3342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386433} {:answer/id #uuid "74e07110-fee2-57d3-a642-c06c3a73ad70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386434} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3cfca1-5908-516d-b059-28896f2cda16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386435} {:answer/id #uuid "b36c6634-4001-5695-9ee5-b01ec38ac4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+2x+2y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386436} {:answer/id #uuid "78982c37-dd15-5cfc-a4b2-ec194bcb56a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc3cfca1-5908-516d-b059-28896f2cda16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386435} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc1582c5-7435-5ca4-898e-aba8007cc959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c85ae37-614c-52e2-a347-0d9aae387c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-109600" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b6de7829e702377d1b20bb1b297d3d28e6fb8bae9672b0137d92b4d53a96c83.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086ff0d3-9802-5b68-a9cb-41dafa67d967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}+3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546265} {:answer/id #uuid "30a9c9a0-3a60-59e3-914b-86e0c5ef4c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (3\\sqrt{3}-2)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546357} {:answer/id #uuid "60ad6e24-752a-5c4f-807f-3d9a1b79aa4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}+1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546459} {:answer/id #uuid "e78fc2a7-f5a9-5a81-9b4f-086b95b49f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (5\\sqrt{5}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546769} {:answer/id #uuid "ef15e05d-4494-53c4-8c45-b01a9fca320f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e78fc2a7-f5a9-5a81-9b4f-086b95b49f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (5\\sqrt{5}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546769} :answer-field/id #uuid "010dbb7c-fb65-531f-9b56-64aba3661590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29d4c247-c6fe-5732-b0d8-fc8ae1646f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109763" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0faa92e0f89c86248abffad7f61cac5d94a3848a7f7be4464c4b28d3915aa32.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[{5}^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (5\\sqrt{5}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3e776d0-51e7-5553-8d41-8b0a54fa611a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174053} {:answer/id #uuid "3eda6ec4-d38b-53da-85d2-5714a3e3f260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174054} {:answer/id #uuid "655e1665-c683-526c-84bf-17daaff44b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x + 8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174055} {:answer/id #uuid "75b3821c-5264-5fda-ba5f-04f332e4d18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174056} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8a48eb-8441-5268-bad4-1f8db5fc1ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174057}] :answer-field/id #uuid "83c1987c-d618-5baf-b5ad-c626fdba3228" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174052}] :question/id #uuid "8d40347d-4268-5d8a-b1fa-86eeedc4c297" :question/math-academy-id "q-118043" :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{x + 3}{x + 2})^{4x + 8}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41f1fafd-d827-531f-8c70-6919b2012ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480220} {:answer/id #uuid "5c52ccdf-bc66-5508-a679-0e1f1417b58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480267} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2fd2a0-35b0-5c42-b70a-f84a88d2104e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480315} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddc2f7c-ba78-53a1-9db1-6d1f0654ddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480370} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7d8a12-8d4e-52c1-ba72-0806b3d500b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c2fd2a0-35b0-5c42-b70a-f84a88d2104e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480315} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c0cd21-a7b1-553c-a1a1-665667bbf015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b6ee570-37f7-5702-8807-dc4014b04274" :question/math-academy-id "q-128675" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{9}{4}$ as a base raised to a negative exponent." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{9}{4}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{2\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{3}{2}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248f0c77-27a3-5962-98e6-5d14c1f94958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=\\infty $ and $I=(-\\infty ,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529659} {:answer/id #uuid "60509868-fe58-5055-98d4-86f8dd815071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529814} {:answer/id #uuid "ac66bd7b-5f2c-5f83-b234-a44f9c1dabb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530009} {:answer/id #uuid "c505fa40-bc71-5bac-b182-da34ecdbd863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(0,2]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530073} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5f0831-9451-54cb-98ea-9585f500774d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[0,2]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac66bd7b-5f2c-5f83-b234-a44f9c1dabb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530009} :answer-field/id #uuid "18b5f7fa-12be-5593-adf1-618dd4567c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1641b66b-2024-5a8a-bd3c-3f7b1ce8b424" :question/math-academy-id "q-13956" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2(n+1)-1}}{2(n+1)-1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2n+1}}{2n+1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n-1}{2n+1}) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(2-\\frac{1}{n})}{(2+\\frac{1}{n})}) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1{|}^{2} & <1 \\\\ |x-1| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-1 & <1 \\\\ 0<x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(0,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x-1|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{((0)-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-⋯ \\\\  & =-(1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+⋯)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series in parentheses with ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},$ we see that the series is divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- When $x=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{((2)-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},$ we see that the series is divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=(0,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "638f95eb-fe03-5c9c-b325-ab64d1a2bfb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-2\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435566} {:answer/id #uuid "838c6fad-c2ba-572a-946c-b271606a25e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\theta }^{16}\\,{e}^{-18\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435572} {:answer/id #uuid "c60c1729-d011-5a55-ab06-3140f2535dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-18\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435580} {:answer/id #uuid "da146878-2071-56de-b976-f02f6e1c86b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435581} {:answer/id #uuid "e63ce25c-9077-5101-b2f1-edcd452a4672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e63ce25c-9077-5101-b2f1-edcd452a4672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435585} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1803602-4fb6-5082-a851-eb54e4e8a4d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab6f3ebb-34cb-52f6-a546-298076d55271" :question/math-academy-id "q-143472" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}=18,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{16}({x}_{i})!=6.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=18,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=6,$ and $n=16.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e6f0911-fed8-5e24-b9b4-bef6d35fd7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi (2\\sqrt{13}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276321} {:answer/id #uuid "60a4a5b1-3058-5b06-8039-c37c71857db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{13}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276343} {:answer/id #uuid "61bb69ac-10e3-5354-9b67-b442dd132871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (\\sqrt{15}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276358} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff11e4e-b692-54a2-9e94-8012f71c93ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (13\\sqrt{13}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276638} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e75565-8620-54e3-b9fb-098b592cc9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (\\sqrt{15}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff11e4e-b692-54a2-9e94-8012f71c93ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (13\\sqrt{13}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276638} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bf03c7d-6f4f-5c6c-a66e-f8472edbf199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b708a90c-5d84-533d-ba0c-156466010875" :question/math-academy-id "q-161178" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the paraboloid $x=5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2},\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 3},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66b05919a7644818eea7265ff3ae8fc3ced4ac7d1d6d30352f612649393c876a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{13}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{13}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(13)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (13\\sqrt{13}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5620b61-2f72-51ef-bb63-b890e672d206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174733} {:answer/id #uuid "c484724d-c629-5ab3-842d-6f0a9400a293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174734} {:answer/id #uuid "cb70f1be-8740-5c4a-9cfc-e5f1a5444017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174735} {:answer/id #uuid "f1645ce1-ec6b-5ae1-8aad-194e219db2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174736} {:answer/id #uuid "1141671d-f6e4-53d6-b900-1076435b632a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174737}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ffde6a17-a74e-55c3-9961-e3fa6a5dc959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174732}] :question/id #uuid "8ff187ed-a6b7-5257-ac7f-a1769b826b13" :question/math-academy-id "q-210" :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{1}{2})^{-3}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fb93c62-cdac-5727-8eb0-645cd00888f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174761} {:answer/id #uuid "b548d60f-b150-56f2-ba2e-a99af733b88f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174762} {:answer/id #uuid "d16853b4-8289-51b9-99c5-f1f36c1651fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174763} {:answer/id #uuid "0bedcc68-7443-5600-8716-1f80b5536477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174764} {:answer/id #uuid "20c9bca3-4ba9-5975-afc8-3353e7c62bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #